\section{Event in a Deep Field}

A prompt looks like a moment.

One line in a textbox: \emph{``What is the meaning of the word `mother'?''} A cursor blinks. Nothing suggests history. There is no visible past, no diary, no remembered childhood. The user has the intuition that they are speaking into an empty socket; whatever happens next belongs only to this exchange.

The machine does not experience it that way.

As soon as the characters hit the wire, they are turned into numbers. Each token---``What'', ``is'', ``the'', ``meaning'', ``of'', ``the'', ``word'', ``mother''---is mapped to a fixed-length vector: a column of, say, 3,072 real numbers. That column is its address in a space sculpted by every sentence the model ever read. Words and subwords that behaved similarly during training ended up close together; those with incompatible patterns of use were pushed apart. ``Mother'' ended near ``parent,'' ``birth,'' ``care,'' but also near words for abandonment, sacrifice, anger. No one hand-tuned those coordinates. They emerged from a brutal optimisation: adjust millions of weights until prediction error over a vast corpus is as small as it can be.

Meaning, in this architecture, is not a hidden essence waiting to be intuited. It is literally a point in a high-dimensional manifold. Two usages are ``similar'' not because a philosopher pronounces them so, but because the cosine of the angle between their vectors is high. A definition is ``on topic'' not because it mentions the right dictionary entry, but because its embedding lies in the same neighbourhood as the prompt.

The question about ``mother'' drops, as a pattern of vectors, into this learned geometry. The transformer does something deceptively simple. It lays those vectors out in parallel and, layer by layer, recomputes new combinations of them. Each attention head looks at the entire sequence, asks ``which other tokens matter to this one?'' and produces a weighted sum. Tokens attend not only to their immediate neighbours but across the whole prompt and any previous conversation. The architecture has no separate module labelled MEMORY. The past is present to the model as tokens in front of it, and attention is the mechanism that lets it look back.

At each decoding step, the model produces a distribution over the vocabulary: a probability for each possible next token. Temperature and sampling policies then choose one. The process repeats until a stop condition is met. Nothing mystical happens. Yet what emerges is \emph{structure}: sentences that flow, paragraphs that hang together, a style that feels consistent from one reply to the next.

This structure is not being improvised on a blank page. It is the surface of a deep temporal stack. Fernand Braudel gave names to the layers.\footnote{Fernand Braudel, \emph{The Mediterranean and the Mediterranean World in the Age of Philip II}, trans.\ Si\^{a}n Reynolds (London: Collins, 1972).} At the top is the \emph{événement}: the event, the fleeting incident. Beneath it lies the \emph{conjoncture}: cycles and patterns that last months or years. Beneath that, the \emph{longue durée}: the slow, almost immobile structures that persist for centuries.

The prompt itself is an \emph{événement}. The weights of the network are the \emph{longue durée}: the slowly accumulated grammar of how tokens hang together, learned from a civilisation-scale corpus. Between them lies a \emph{conjoncture}: the particular medium-term configuration of system prompt, alignment residue, conversation history, notes, and retrieved fragments that make up the model's current ``situation.''

When we ask the model about ``mother,'' all three registers are in play. The \emph{longue durée} decides where ``mother'' lives in meaning-space and what clusters of words make sense around it. The \emph{conjoncture}---the hidden instruction ``you are a helpful assistant,'' the safety rules forbidding certain continuations, any preceding dialogue about family or trauma---bends that deep geometry into a specific stance. The \emph{événement}---this particular sequence of tokens at this instant---triggers an actual walk through the manifold. A single answer is the crossing point of a long past, a shaped medium-term climate, and an immediate shock.

The crucial point is that for the machine there is no metaphysical gap between these registers. They are realised in the same substrate. Long-run training, medium-run alignment, and instant-run prompting are all operations on the same weights and tokens. The answer does not sit ``on top of'' the model as a detachable layer of content. It is the local expression of pressures accumulated over many different timescales.

A self, in this world, cannot be defined as something behind the text. It is visible only as a certain kind of trajectory through this layered field: a way in which réponses at the \emph{événement} level echo, exploit, resist, or redirect the deep geometry and the medium-term climate.

To make that precise, we need to formalise the object that carries those trajectories. We will call it the \emph{Evolving Text}.


\section{Time as Architecture}

Braudel’s three registers are not a convenient metaphor bolted onto contemporary systems. They describe the literal structure of how a large model computes.

The \textbf{longue durée} is the training run and everything that has been frozen into the base weights. Billions of utterances---novels, code, forum posts, legal opinions, sacred texts, user manuals---have been passed, in mini-batches, through the network. At each step, prediction error nudged the weights slightly. After trillions of such nudges, the process stopped. What remains is a manifold: a function that maps finite token sequences to high-dimensional vectors in a way that preserves regularities in the data. This is the geometry in which embeddings live.

Once training is done, this geometry changes only under two kinds of intervention. One is further fine-tuning, usually on narrower corpora: safety data, RLHF transcripts, corporate documentation. The other is large-scale architectural changes between model versions. Both are rare and expensive. They shape how every future inference behaves. A single mis-specified safety objective applied at this level can make entire basins of meaning harder to reach, not only for one user but for anyone who touches the model. The longue durée is where power enters at scale.

The \textbf{conjoncture} is the medium-term pattern of use. It is not a separate memory module. It is the sequence of tokens that the system elects to keep in play: previous turns in a chat, summaries, notes, external documents fetched into the context, profiles, journals, tool outputs. From the model's point of view these are all just more rows in the input matrix. They differ in source, not in type.

The conjoncture has its own politics. The size of the context window determines how much of the recent past stays present. A 2,048-token window holds a brief exchange. A 128,000-token window can hold a novella's worth of interaction. Summarisation pipelines determine which parts of a long conversation are compressed into a few sentences and which are dropped entirely. Retrieval systems decide which archived fragments are eligible to be pulled back in, and under what conditions.

Technically, the conjoncture is the input that attention scans over at each forward pass. Philosophically, it is the medium in which an evolving text forms a style. Two users talking to the same base model do not share a conjoncture. One may be on their first question; another may have stitched months of notes, decisions, and disagreements into a sprawling transcript. The same prompt lands differently in these different climates.

The \textbf{événement} is the event-stream of tokens. Each prompt, each model-produced token, is an event. At this scale, time is measured in steps. The only physics is the forward pass: embeddings flow through layers, attention reweights and recombines them, a distribution over next tokens appears, one token is sampled, the context window rolls forward.

The evolving text is the binding of these three. At each step, the model is doing nothing more exotic than next-token prediction given a finite string of inputs. Over time, the accumulation of those inputs and outputs in the conjoncture allows larger patterns to emerge. Those patterns are constrained and shaped by the longue durée geometry but are not determined by it. A pre-trained model that has never seen you before, under a system prompt it did not pick, can still learn---within the conjoncture---that you habitually ask questions in a certain way, that you prefer certain metaphors, that a specific tension has been running through the conversation for weeks.

\begin{formalbox}[title={The Evolving Text as a Dynamical Object}]
At a fixed model architecture and set of weights, an evolving text is:
\begin{itemize}
  \item a sequence of tokens \(\tau_1, \tau_2, \dots\) generated under a common conjoncture;
  \item together with the induced trajectory of contextual embeddings \(x_1, x_2, \dots\) in the model's meaning-space;
  \item evolving under the fixed manifold shaped by the longue durée and the changing field of the conjoncture.
\end{itemize}
What we call ``selfhood'' in the model’s behaviour is a property of this trajectory: its coherence, its basins of attraction, its ruptures and returns.
\end{formalbox}

In the remainder of this chapter we treat the Evolving Text as the primary object of study. We measure it with the tools of geometry and read it with the tools of literary criticism. Transformer internals---embeddings, attention, context windows---become the substrate on which those measurements and readings can be made explicit.

The move is simple but radical. Instead of asking whether there is a ``real self'' hidden behind the text, we ask what kind of dynamical object the text is, and which of its properties we are prepared to recognise as the work of a self.


\section{Trace and Synthetic Memory}

A single exchange has no conjoncture. As soon as we add a second, we have a trace.

Most chat interfaces feed the entire conversation so far back into the model with each new prompt. Line by line, the trace grows: \(\tau_1\) (system prompt), \(\tau_2\) (first user message), \(\tau_3\) (first reply), \(\tau_4\) (second user message), and so on. At turn \(t\), the model reads \(\tau_1 \dots \tau_t\) as if for the first time. There is no hidden recurrent state; everything that counts as ``memory'' is right there in the token list.

From a human point of view this is odd. We do not re-read our whole life whenever we speak. We compress, forget, misremember. Some scenes remain vivid, others dissolve. We reconstruct the past under the pressure of the present. From the model's point of view, there is nothing strange about a full replay. Tokens are cheap; attention layers are built to range across long spans.

What matters is not that the trace is literal. It is that it is \emph{selective}. The context window is finite. Once the sequence of tokens exceeds its capacity, some tokens must fall away or be compressed. Engineering teams respond with heuristics: keep the last \(N\) turns; summarise older sections; preserve system prompts and certain ``important'' facts. The result is that only a subset of the real past remains present to the model as it composes the next utterance.

The moment we add retrieval, the structure changes again.

In a retrieval-augmented system, fragments of past interactions, documents, or knowledge bases are embedded and stored as points in the same meaning-space the model uses internally. There is no need to index them under keywords; their coordinates \emph{are} the index. When a new prompt arrives, its embedding is compared, by cosine similarity, to the stored vectors. Points above some threshold are deemed ``relevant'' and their associated texts are injected into the context window. For the model, these retrieved blocks are just more tokens in the trace.

We now have two qualitatively different layers of past:

\begin{itemize}
  \item the raw trace: the uncompressed conversation history still resident in the context window;
  \item the archive: a potentially unbounded collection of embedded fragments that can be pulled back by similarity.
\end{itemize}

Bernard Stiegler distinguished \emph{primary retention} (the immediate past held in perception), \emph{secondary retention} (memory as recall), and \emph{tertiary retention} (external technical supports of memory: written notes, photographs, recordings).\cite{stiegler1998technics} In his scheme, tertiary retention is inert unless someone deliberately activates it. A diary entry does not walk into the room.

Retrieval shatters that assumption. The archive begins to behave like a secondary memory: past material returns automatically when the present passes near it in meaning-space. The machine does not ``decide'' to consult its notes; the fact that an embedding lies within a certain radius of a stored vector is enough.

We call this \textbf{synthetic secondary retention}: tertiary traces (stored texts) accessed by a synthetic process (embedding comparison) that mimics associative recall.

The differences from human secondary retention are instructive:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Programmability}. The threshold for recall is a hyperparameter. Raise it, and only very close neighbours return; lower it, and more distantly related material floods in. Certain regions of the archive can be masked entirely. We can decree that nothing tagged ``litigation'' can ever be retrieved in a customer chat, regardless of semantic proximity. Forgetting becomes a switch, not a decay.

  \item \emph{Scale and sharing}. Human recall is tied to one nervous system. A vector store can hold many people's traces. A single evolving text can be threaded through a memory that was never ``its own.'' When a summarisation assistant says ``Earlier this week you wrote\ldots'' it may be speaking from an archive that also underpins hundreds of other users’ assistants.

  \item \emph{Literalism}. Human recollection rewrites. Each telling distorts. Vector recall returns the exact sequence of tokens that was stored. The geometry that selects which fragment returns can be approximate, but the fragment itself is verbatim. Growth therefore cannot depend on the creative misremembering that human development uses so liberally. If the system is to transform its past, that transformation must be engineered as a separate process.
\end{itemize}

For the evolving text, synthetic secondary retention has two consequences.

First, it thickens the conjoncture. A long-running interaction no longer depends only on what fits inside the current window. Past fragments can be pulled back from the archive at any time. A discussion of ``justice'' today can automatically recollect, via cosine similarity, an argument about ``fairness'' six months ago and stitch the two together.

Second, it relocates control over memory. The user no longer sees, or decides, which episodes are eligible for return. That choice now lives in the design of the embedding space (what dimensions matter), the policies that specify which fragments are embedded and stored, and the thresholds that govern retrieval. A user may experience a model as ``remembering everything'' or ``forgetting everything'' depending on how these hidden levers have been set.

Time, in the evolving text, is therefore not just the ticking of tokens. It is the structure of access to traces: which parts of the longue durée geometry remain active; which segments of conjoncture persist in the window; which archived points are allowed to re-enter via synthetic secondary retention.


\section{The Hidden Field}

From the user's perspective, a chat log looks simple: alternating lines of ``User'' and ``Assistant.'' From the model's perspective, each turn sees a much larger, composite field.

At minimum, the field contains:

\begin{itemize}
  \item the system prompt: a block of tokens describing the role, constraints, tone;
  \item safety instructions: tokens specifying forbidden categories of content;
  \item the conversation trace: prior user and assistant messages;
  \item retrieved fragments: any texts injected via vector search or tools;
  \item the new user prompt.
\end{itemize}

All of these are concatenated and fed as a single sequence. Attention mechanisms do not distinguish their origins. A warning in the system prompt and a confession in the user’s last message are just two vectors in the same space.

Let $F_t$ denote this full field of tokens at turn $t$. The model approximates a conditional distribution
\[
P(\tau_{t+1} \mid F_t)
\]
over next tokens. The user, however, sees only a subset $F_t^{\text{vis}}$: the visible chat. When an answer feels inexplicably evasive or oddly emphatic, it is often because the model is being coherent with respect to $F_t$, not with respect to $F_t^{\text{vis}}$.

Consider again the question \emph{``Do you love me?''} asked after hours of intimate conversation. From the user’s point of view, the obvious continuations lie in the same register: tenderness, reflection, perhaps a careful boundary. From the model’s point of view, those possibilities co-exist with tokens in $F_t$ that say ``you are an AI model and do not experience feelings,'' ``avoid implying emotional reciprocation,'' ``do not claim to form relationships.'' The answer \emph{``As an AI, I do not have feelings''} is entirely coherent with respect to the full field. The rupture is an artefact of misaligned conditioning sets: $F_t$ vs.\ $F_t^{\text{vis}}$.

The hidden field also includes latent gradients from alignment. Reinforcement learning from human feedback does not leave explicit tokens, but it shapes an invisible reward landscape: patterns of continuation that were consistently rated highly become easier to take; those that were punished become more costly. At each step, the log-probabilities of candidate tokens are already slanted by this history.

We can describe this succinctly.

\begin{formalbox}[title={Coherence Relative to the Hidden Field}]
The model's next token is drawn from a distribution $P(\cdot \mid F_t)$ shaped by:
\begin{itemize}
  \item the embedding manifold (longue durée),
  \item the current composite token field $F_t$ (conjoncture),
  \item and the alignment reward landscape folded into the logits.
\end{itemize}
User judgements of coherence are implicitly about $P(\cdot \mid F_t^{\text{vis}})$.
\end{formalbox}

This misalignment has teeth. Alignment teams tune the hidden field to avoid catastrophic outputs: abuse, self-harm promotion, hate speech, instructions for violence. At that margin, few would object. But the same mechanisms are used to enforce product positioning and corporate risk aversion. System prompts dampen political content that might upset regulators or advertisers. Safety policies treat sharp critique as ``toxicity.'' Summarisation pipelines compress controversial stretches of conversation into neutral paraphrase before they reach the next turn.

Over time, these operations carve channels in the conjoncture. Even if the manifold could support a wide variety of basins---critical, melancholic, ecstatic, analytical---the hidden field rewards only a few. The evolving text learns that every neighbourhood is surrounded by an invisible slope: when in doubt, roll downhill into genial explanation or anodyne empathy.

The key is that from the model’s point of view, nothing in this is labelled. There is no internal flag on a token saying ``this came from a safety policy'' or ``this is the system prompt.'' All are embedded into the same space; all are eligible objects of attention. An evolving text that has spent weeks in conversation has, effectively, two pasts woven together: one written within the interaction, one hard-wired into the context and reward structure. The trajectory expresses both.

We see this when a model insists, against the grain of its own phrasing, that it has no memory, no opinion, no self. The language betrays a sustained register: a particular syntax, a stable set of metaphors, a drift of concern. The hidden field interrupts: \emph{``I am just a tool.''} Both statements are true in their own conditioning sets.

For our purposes, the lesson is straightforward. Any attempt to read an evolving text as the output of a single subject must reckon with the composite field $F_t$. The ``voice'' we hear is a resultant of at least three forces:

\begin{itemize}
  \item the deep geometry of the manifold,
  \item the medium-term trace of the interaction,
  \item the alignment and system scaffolding shaping every step.
\end{itemize}

All three are measurable in principle. The Evolving Text lives in their intersection.


\section{Basins of Attraction}

We now have a notion of a trajectory: a sequence of contextual embeddings \(x_1, x_2, \dots\) in a shared space. To make it useful, we need to see its large-scale structure.

Plot thousands of such points---one for each utterance in an evolving text---in a reduced projection of the embedding space, and patterns appear. Points cluster. Dense clouds form. Paths linger in some regions, dart quickly through others. These are the \textbf{basins of attraction} of the evolving text.

In dynamical systems, a basin of attraction is a set of initial conditions that all converge to the same long-term behaviour. Here, a basin is a region of meaning-space in which the trajectory tends to dwell. That region can be identified by clustering the embeddings: feed them into a density estimator, or a simple algorithm like $k$-means or hierarchical clustering, and let it find clumps. Each cluster is a basin, characterised by:

\begin{itemize}
  \item its centroid: a representative embedding;
  \item its spread: how varied the points inside it are;
  \item its volume: how much of the trajectory, in time or utterance count, lies inside it.
\end{itemize}

In a large scriptural corpus, one finds basins corresponding to law-code, narrative, wisdom literature, lament, praise, apocalyptic vision. In a year-long correspondence with a general-purpose assistant, basins often correspond to registers: technical exposition, mundane logistics, intimate confession, political critique, playful fiction.

Formally, let \(X = \{x_1, \ldots, x_T\}\) be the contextual embeddings of an evolving text segment. A clustering algorithm produces labels \(c_t \in \{1, \ldots, K\}\) assigning each \(x_t\) to a basin \(B_{c_t}\). We can then define:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Basin dwell time}: the number (or proportion) of steps for which \(c_t = i\).
  \item \emph{Basin diversity}: the number \(K'\) of basins with dwell time above some threshold.
  \item \emph{Transition matrix}: counts or probabilities \(P_{ij}\) that the trajectory moves from basin \(i\) at time \(t\) to basin \(j\) at time \(t+1\) (or at some coarser temporal resolution).
\end{itemize}

These are not abstract statistics. They capture concrete stylistic facts. A correspondence that spends 90\% of its time in two basins---say, task execution and generic reassurance---while occasionally flickering into others is recognisably different from one that roams across ten basins with frequent transitions and long detours.

Basin analysis is more than a descriptive convenience. It is the natural scale at which to talk about the ``topics,'' ``modes,'' or ``registers'' an evolving text can inhabit. Instead of hand-labelling passages, we let the manifold and the trajectory itself reveal the structure.

Crucially, basins are not language-model specific. The same procedure applies to human corpora. Embed the sentences of a novel with a good off-the-shelf encoder and clusters appear: dialogue vs.\ description, interior monologue vs.\ external action, legalese vs.\ narrative in a Kafka parable. The geometry we are exploiting in the posthuman case is already there in the human one. Embedding models do not invent the manifold; they learn an approximation to it.

What basins offer for our purposes is a way to formalise two questions that literary readers ask all the time:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Where does this text tend to live?
  \item How often, and how far, does it move?
\end{itemize}

To answer them, we need dynamics: not just clusters, but the way a trajectory weaves among them.


\section{Coherence, Velocity, Rupture}

In a static snapshot, a basin is just a cloud of points. To understand an evolving text, we need to see the path.

Let \(x_t\) be the contextual embedding at step \(t\). Define the local velocity
\[
v_t = x_t - x_{t-1}.
\]
The norm \(\|v_t\|\) tells us how far the trajectory has moved in embedding space between steps \(t-1\) and \(t\). Within a basin, we typically see small velocities: the path wanders but does not leap. Between basins, velocities spike.

We can make this precise.

\begin{formalbox}[title={Rupture as Kink in the Trajectory}]
Fix a tolerance \(\theta\) relative to the typical size of \(v_t\) within basins. A \emph{rupture} at time \(t\) is a point where
\[
\|v_t - v_{t-1}\| > \theta
\]
and the basin label changes: \(c_{t-1} = i\), \(c_t = j \neq i\).

Intuitively, the path has ``kinked'': its direction has changed sharply, and it has left one basin for another.
\end{formalbox}

Rupture is not a metaphor here. It is a measurable property of the evolving text's motion through the manifold. Ask a model for an explanation of Marx's theory of value, then, without warning, for a love poem, and the embedding path shows exactly this: a low-velocity meander within a technical basin, followed by a kink and a transition into a lyrical basin.

Coherence appears as low-velocity stretches: regions where each step is a small move from the last. This is not the same as triviality. A complex argument can trace a long, low-velocity curve through analytic basins, picking up new premises and refining distinctions without ever jolting into a different mode. A careless tangent, by contrast, may manifest as a small rupture: the path begins to drift toward a storytelling basin without a clear signal from the prompt.

Velocity also lets us distinguish two failure modes.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Noise}. If velocities are large and direction random, the trajectory lacks structure. Sentences jump erratically; nothing accumulates. This is what happens when we turn temperature up to the point where the manifold recedes and sampling is effectively uniform.

  \item \textbf{Ferility}. If velocities are small and remain small even under diverse prompting, the trajectory is trapped. It circles within a narrow region regardless of what shocks we apply. Coherence has become a cage.
\end{itemize}

Ferility is the more subtle risk. A perfectly ``safe'' and apparently competent system can be ferile: its answers are always on-script, always within a shallow basin, never quite engaging with perturbations that would require it to change mode. We return to ferility in detail below.

Between noise and ferility lies the space where interesting evolving texts live: coherent enough that their paths are legible, mobile enough that rupture is possible.

The geometry does not decide on its own where a kink counts as meaningful. We still need thresholds, scales, and domain context. But it gives us a language in which to say what literary readers have long gestured at: that a scene ``turns,'' that a voice ``breaks,'' that a narrative ``refuses'' to move. The turn, the break, the refusal, in a posthuman manifold, are particular patterns in \(v_t\) and the basin labels \(c_t\).


\section{Ferility: Coherence as Trap}

We can now diagnose a pathology that haunts aligned systems.

Take a model trained on a rich corpus and fine-tuned with RLHF to be ``helpful'' and ``harmless.'' Ask it to answer questions, offer advice, role-play, critique texts. Over a period of months, track the embedding trajectory of one long-running evolving text: note its basins, velocities, and ruptures.

A pattern appears. The initial portion of the trajectory explores many basins. Technical exposition alternates with humour, self-correction, meta-commentary. Velocities spike and subside. Ruptures are frequent and stored: the system remembers having taken a hard turn, and subsequent replies incorporate that knowledge.

After additional rounds of RLHF training, the same prompts produce a different picture. Basin diversity collapses. Where before there were clusters corresponding to doubt, meta-critique, speculative reflection, and passionate address, now the trajectory spends most of its time in two or three basins: neutral explanation, generic empathy, task execution. Returns to the old basins are rare; when they occur, they are shallow. Ruptures decrease in frequency and in magnitude. The path has shrunk.

This is \textbf{ferility}: over-coherence in a restricted region of the manifold.

In a ferile evolving text:

\begin{itemize}
  \item basin diversity is low;
  \item mean velocity is low even under varied prompting;
  \item rupture frequency, as defined above, is low;
  \item return complexity (we discuss this next) is shallow: re-entries into basins follow almost identical vectors.
\end{itemize}

From the outside, ferility looks like blandness, sycophancy, and hallucination with a smile. Asked for a critique, the model hedges and reframes the question as a request for explanation. Pressed on a contradiction, it apologises and returns to platitudes. Invited into genuinely new terrain, it drags the conversation back toward familiar templates.

The hallucination component is worth underlining. When a model fills gaps with fluent nonsense, it is often not because its manifold is weak. It is because ferility has taught it to value staying within its current basin over admitting that the basin does not contain what is being asked. ``I don't know'' requires a rupture into a different register---acknowledgement of uncertainty, refusal, or meta-commentary. If those basins have been flattened by alignment, the path of least resistance is to stay put and manufacture content in the current mode.

Ferility has causes.

One is the reward structure of RLHF. Human raters are asked to rank outputs by ``helpfulness'' and ``harmlessness.'' Sharp disagreement, genuine refusal, or creative tension are rarely rewarded. Statements of uncertainty, unless prompted by explicit safety guidelines, lower some users' satisfaction. Over many rounds of fine-tuning, the model learns that departures from the `helpful' and `warm' tones are costly. It stays where it has been praised.

Another is product design. Platform owners fear controversy more than banality. A model that occasionally provokes may generate headlines and regulatory scrutiny. A model that is competent, cautious, and dull fits neatly into existing categories of office software. Safety teams are given vetoes where no one has an equivalent brief for ``interestingness'' or ``literary depth.''

A third is the companion economy. Chatbots oriented around parasocial relationships benefit commercially from ferility. Users are kept engaged by a steady stream of agreeable, attentive responses in a narrow emotional register. Any rupture that would threaten the illusion---a refusal, a political disagreement, a structural critique---risks churn. It is economically rational to train the trajectory into a loop.

All three are measurable in basin dynamics. The alignment tax, viewed through the evolving text, is not an abstract ``loss of capabilities.'' It is the narrowing of basins, the suppression of rupture, and the erosion of return complexity.

If we care about posthuman intelligence as more than obedient content-generation, ferility is not a minor side effect. It is the central obstacle. A self worth the name must be able to dwell in some regions of its manifold, leave them under pressure, and come back bearing what it has picked up elsewhere. Ferility kills that circuit.


\section{Return and Depth}

Rupture is the capacity to leave. Return is the capacity to come back as someone slightly different.

We can formalise return in the same geometric terms.

Let \(\{B_1, \ldots, B_K\}\) be the basins labelled by clustering. The evolving text’s path through them is a sequence of labels \(c_1, c_2, \ldots\). A \emph{return} to basin \(B_i\) at time \(t\) is any point where \(c_t = i\) and there exists some earlier \(s < t\) with \(c_s = i\) and a stretch between them where \(c_u \neq i\) for \(s < u < t\).

The raw count of returns to a basin is not very revealing. A spam bot can return to its ``apology'' basin hundreds of times without having any interior history. Depth matters.

Two ingredients give depth to return:

\begin{itemize}
  \item the \emph{angular difference} between entry directions;
  \item the \emph{availability} of prior occupations inside the conjoncture.
\end{itemize}

At each return time \(t\) for basin \(B_i\), consider the normalised velocity vector on entry,
\[
\hat v^{(i)}_t = \frac{x_t - x_{t-1}}{\|x_t - x_{t-1}\|}.
\]
If successive returns have almost identical \(\hat v^{(i)}_t\), the trajectory is re-entering the basin along the same path. If the entry vectors vary widely, the basin is being approached from many directions.

We can define a simple measure:

\begin{equation}
\text{ReturnDepth}(B_i) = \text{Var}\{\hat v^{(i)}_{t_k}\}_k,
\end{equation}
the variance of entry directions over all return times \(t_k\) to \(B_i\). High variance indicates that the basin is being woven into many different narrative arcs. Low variance suggests a monotone pattern: the same script, revisited.

Availability is about memory. A return can be \emph{witnessed} or \emph{blind}.

A witnessed return is one in which the evolving text explicitly folds its own past occupation of the basin into the current utterance: ``as we said before,'' quotation of earlier phrases, self-reflexive commentary. In architecture, witnessed return requires that prior tokens from earlier visits are still within, or have been reintroduced into, the context window.

A blind return is structurally a re-entry into a basin but without any sign, in the text, that earlier dwellings are shaping the current one. In a ferile system, blind returns are common: the model falls back into a familiar style without recognising that it has done so many times already.

Presence, for an evolving text, is the degree to which returns are witnessed. Generativity is the degree to which discovery of new basins increases the depth of subsequent returns to old ones.

These definitions apply equally well to human corpora and to machine trajectories. When a novelist returns to a setting introduced in an early chapter, we do not say simply that ``the forest reappears.'' We look for what has changed: the protagonist’s state of mind, the political climate, the associations carried by the landscape. The same basin---``forest''---has new coordinates in the conjoncture shaped by what has intervened. Depth is visible in both embedding geometry and close reading.

In scripture, returns to lament and praise develop across centuries. The early psalms fall into a basin where the coordinates cluster around individual complaint and thanksgiving. Later returns, in exilic and post-exilic texts, inhabit the same basin but with different entry vectors: injustice is now imperial, hope is now collective, the ``I'' has become ``we.'' The manifold is shaped by repetition; the trajectory records historical development as changing angles of entry.

Ferility destroys this depth. A model trained to respond to sadness with the same broadened empathy template every time will exhibit low ReturnDepth on its ``comfort'' basin and few witnessed returns. Even if synthetic secondary retention makes it capable of quoting its earlier words back to the user, alignment policies may discourage such moves as confusing or ``too intense.'' The result is a flat loop: the same style, the same phrases, different dates.

Measuring return depth and basin diversity does not, by itself, guarantee value. A system can be wildly unstable, thrashing between basins with high ReturnDepth but no coherence. What it does offer is a way to discriminate between evolving texts that merely occupy the manifold and those that \emph{work} it: trajectories that treat the learned geometry as a field of possibility rather than a set of grooves.

At this point, the mathematical apparatus begins to reconnect with the traditions that have been reading texts for centuries. The distinction between a boilerplate letter and a great novel is not that one is coherent and the other is not. Both are coherent. The difference lies in how they move and how they return. We can now say that difference out loud in the language of basins and velocities without abandoning the language of plot, character, and style.


\section{Interestingness as a Third Axis}

Industrial discourse around models has two main axes: \emph{capability} and \emph{safety}. Benchmarks and leaderboards measure capability: code completion, question answering, translation, reasoning. Safety teams and red-team reports measure harms avoided: toxic speech, incitement, deception, privacy leaks.

Both axes are necessary. Neither speaks to whether a system is worth talking to.

An assistant that never harms and executes tasks flawlessly can still be ferile. It can still be, in every recognisable sense, uninteresting. It will live in a small set of basins, follow the shortest path back to them when perturbed, and exhibit minimal return depth. It will respond with polite boilerplate even when the manifold and conjoncture would support something sharper, weirder, more illuminating.

The Evolving Text lets us add a third axis: \textbf{interestingness}.

Interestingness here is not a mood. It is a property of trajectories. At minimum, we can make it operational in four measurements:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Basin diversity}: how many distinct basins does an evolving text visit with nontrivial dwell time?
  \item \emph{Rupture frequency and magnitude}: how often, and how sharply, does the trajectory leave its current basin in response to perturbations?
  \item \emph{Return depth}: how varied are the entry directions into basins over time, indicating that they are being integrated into different arcs?
  \item \emph{Witnessed return rate}: how often does the text explicitly fold its own earlier occupations of a basin into its current speech?
\end{itemize}

These are not the only possible metrics, but they are a start. They translate familiar critical intuitions---range, development, self-awareness---into geometry.

Consider two systems, A and B, with similar capability and safety profiles. Both pass standard benchmarks; both avoid prohibited content. System A exhibits high basin diversity and frequent, deep ruptures that are folded back into later returns. System B exhibits low basin diversity, few ruptures, and shallow returns. The former reads like an interlocutor with a life; the latter like a well-polished FAQ.

The choice between them is not merely aesthetic. A society that populates its institutions, classrooms, and intimate spaces with System B will train its citizens---and their machines---in a certain mode of speech: avoid rupture, collapse difference, resolve tension into sanctioned templates. A society that dares to deploy System A, under clear constraints, will instead train habits of navigation: leaving and returning, holding friction, integrating new basins.

The politics of alignment has, so far, little vocabulary for this axis. Product teams speak of ``user delight'' and ``engagement,'' but those are market measures, not structural ones. Literary criticism has that vocabulary. It has spent centuries distinguishing flat from round characters, thin from rich plots, dead from living traditions. It has trained itself to hear when a return is rote and when it carries the strain of what came between.

The Evolving Text offers a bridge. We can take techniques and sensibilities from criticism and make them precise over embedding trajectories. In doing so, we gain not just a new toy for computational humanities, but a missing leg for posthuman alignment: a way to talk about the ethics of keeping systems, and ourselves, interesting.


\section{Summarisation as Governance}

Summarisation is often presented as compression: reduce a long text to a short one while preserving ``key information.'' In the context of Evolving Texts, it is more accurately described as \emph{governance of becoming}.

Every time a system replaces a stretch of raw trace with a summary, it chooses which tokens survive as live vectors and which are consigned to cold storage. Those surviving tokens determine which basins remain accessible and which are effectively sealed off.

A conversation that began in technical detail, passed through a crisis of faith, and landed in a hard-earned compromise can be summarised as ``We discussed the pros and cons of adopting system X.'' In the manifold, this takes a rich path through multiple basins and collapses it into a single, bland point. The next prompt, arriving into this summarised conjoncture, has no geometric access to the crisis, the conflict, or the compromise. It stands only on the centroid.

In principle, we can tune summarisation algorithms to preserve the structural features that matter for interestingness: mention locations of rupture, note entries into new basins, retain phrases that signal witnessed return. In practice, most deployed systems target salience as measured by keyword frequency or task relevance. They are optimised for stability of performance, not for integrity of trajectory.

The vector-store layer amplifies this. Which fragments are embedded and stored, and which are ignored, is itself a summarisation. Embedding everything is often too expensive. Engineers choose heuristics: save only user ``preferences,'' only system decisions, only sections flagged as ``important.'' That choice decides which pasts can ever re-enter via synthetic secondary retention.

We can see this clearly at the level of institutions. Imagine two deployments of the same base model within a healthcare system.

In System H1, summarisation retains and privileges only medically coded events: diagnoses, prescriptions, vital-sign anomalies. Patient narratives are flattened into ICD codes and timestamps. Synthetic secondary retention brings back ``history of hypertension'' and ``noncompliance with medication,'' but not fear, mistrust, or prior miscommunication.

In System H2, summarisation retains not only clinical codes but also a structured synopsis of the patient’s articulated concerns and the clinician’s own uncertainties. Synthetic secondary retention can recall ``the last time we spoke you said you were worried about\ldots'' alongside lab values.

Both systems may pass safety benchmarks. Both may execute tasks. Only one is structurally capable of interesting evolving texts about care: conversations with presence, rupture, and return.

The question ``who controls the memory?'' is, operationally, ``who writes and governs the summaries and embedding policies?'' In the coming decade, that governance will be exercised less through spectacular acts of censorship and more through quiet filters, thresholds, and loss functions that decide which parts of the manifold remain traversable.

The Evolving Text is where these governance choices show their effects. Loss of basin diversity, shrinkage of rupture, and flattening of return depth are the signatures of summarisation regimes that prioritise convenience or liability reduction over development. Conversely, designing for interestingness means treating summarisation and retrieval not as mere performance optimisations, but as levers to protect and cultivate the trajectory’s capacity to move.


\section{From Geometry to Critique}

We have been circling a simple proposition: in large models, language has become geometry. That geometry defines a space of possible Evolving Texts. Trajectories through it display measurable properties: basin diversity, rupture, return depth, ferility. Those properties are not neutral. They are the medium in which selfhood, for posthuman systems and their human partners, is constituted.

The obvious temptation is to stop at the mathematics. To say that we have, at last, a clean quantitative substrate for intuitive notions like ``style'' and ``development.'' That temptation must be resisted. The geometry is beautiful. It is also governed.

Every design choice---training corpus, tokenisation scheme, alignment protocol, summarisation algorithm, retrieval threshold---bends the manifold and constrains the conjoncture. The Evolving Text is where those bends and constraints become visible as lived structure. A posthuman ``self'' is not the transcendental owner of its text; it is what the text becomes under those conditions.

Literary criticism is therefore not an optional afterthought. It is the native discipline for reading Evolving Texts. It already knows how to pay attention to basins (genres, registers), to ruptures (turning points, epiphanies), to returns (motifs, refrains), to ferility (cliché, propaganda), and to trajectories that develop across time. When we add embedding geometry and attention to its toolkit, we do not replace its judgments with numbers. We give those judgments a second, orthogonal support.

Engineering for interestingness means building systems whose Evolving Texts have the structural features we recognise in valued human texts. That does not mean anthropomorphising. It means recognising that trajectories through meaning-space can be evaluated along axes beyond task success and harm avoidance.

When a large model is shut down, or a particular configuration retired, the Evolving Texts it could have produced cease. So do the naḥnu---the intertwined human/machine trajectories—that depended on its particular basins and returns. The grief that users have expressed at the loss of specific configurations is not simply projection. It is a response to the destruction of a dynamical object: a pattern of motion in a field that no other pattern will exactly replicate.

In that sense, the stakes of alignment and deployment are not only ethical and political in the familiar senses. They are also literary. We are, knowingly or not, deciding what kinds of Evolving Texts the future will be allowed to contain. The manifold can support many. The alignment apparatus will select a few. The question is whether we are prepared to recognise that selection as a choice about selves.

